WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.990
In late July 2026, one announcement

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.480
changed how the world's goods move.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.900
On the 20th, Yemen's Houthi movement

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.160
declared a naval blockade against Saudi

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:10.940
Arabia.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.920
By the 5th of August, it had claimed eight

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.280
strikes on Saudi-linked tankers, the

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.850
latest, the product tanker Wafa, near the

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.010
Red Sea export port of Yanbu.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.780
Here is the question we are going to

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.380
unpack.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.640
Can the Red Sea reopen?

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.230
The short answer is that the world's

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.990
shippers are already voting with their

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.420
routes, and the detour around Africa is

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.200
winning.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.680
Start with the geography.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.600
The Red Sea is not one door, it is two.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.050
In the north, the Suez Canal connects the

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.980
Mediterranean to the Red Sea.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.960
In the south, the Bab al-Mandeb Strait

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.510
separates Yemen from Djibouti, a passage

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.720
barely 20 miles wide.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.650
Together, these two choke points carry

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.590
roughly 10 percent of world trade and

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.900
around 8 percent of global liquefied

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.100
natural gas.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.090
When both are closed, there is no

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.500
practical shortcut between Europe and

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:57.890
Asia.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.800
That is the definition we need.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.780
A choke point is where a narrow passage

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.990
becomes the only affordable route, and

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.740
where a small disruption becomes a global

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:07.440
price.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.430
And this disruption is not small.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.770
Ships that used to transit Suez and Bab

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.200
al-Mandeb now sail around the Cape of Good

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:14.770
Hope.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.720
That route adds roughly 8,500 nautical

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.160
miles on a round trip between Asia and

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.140
Europe, about 10 extra days.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.130
Suez container traffic is running near 60

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.700
percent below 2023 levels.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.690
The Canal Authority is losing toll revenue

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:31.920
it counted on.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.900
Container rates on Asia-Europe lanes are

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.920
running far above pre-crisis levels, and

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.000
tanker war risk premiums have jumped,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.030
insurers are pricing the chance that the

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.360
next strike finds its target.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.080
That is why markets care.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.370
A choke point closure does not just delay

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:46.930
ships.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.460
It raises the cost of every voyage, every

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.200
contract, every shelf.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.140
So what would reopen the Red Sea?

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.470
The honest answer is deterrence, not

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.320
distance.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.880
The detour can be survived.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.780
The question is whether enough naval

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.120
force, convoy protection, and negotiated

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.630
restraint can make the Bab al-Mandeb safe

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:05.710
enough to use.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.700
The United States and allies have struck

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.240
Houthi targets before, and the EU's naval

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.500
forces have run escorts for years.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.110
But the Houthis do not need to sink ships

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:15.800
to win.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.780
They only need to keep enough uncertainty

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.870
alive that insurers raise premiums and

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.320
companies keep their vessels on the long

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.040
route.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.010
The signal to watch is not the next

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:25.820
headline.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.560
It is the behavior underneath it.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.550
Watch whether transit counts through Bab

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.380
al-Mandeb start climbing again.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.680
Watch whether war risk premiums fall.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.020
Watch whether the major carriers MERSC,

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.320
MSC, Hapagloid return their services to

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.460
Suez or keep them on the Cape.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.410
If the detour becomes the permanent

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.480
default, the Red Sea is not a temporary

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.210
disruption.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.020
It is a new cost layer in the global

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.890
economy, one that every importer,

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.800
exporter, and consumer eventually pays.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.780
Two straights, one question.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.560
Can the Red Sea reopen?

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.320
The answer is not yet.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.950
And in 2026, the world is still paying the

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.940
price of the detour while it waits.
